  Scenario: What moved to the GPU, and what stayed
    Then on the GPU, as K kernels over GPU-resident buffers:
      | stage                                   | kernel                       | before (step 0)                               |
      | YUV to RGB                              | composite.k                  | VideoToolbox BGRA + a CPU swizzle (0.97 ms)  |
      | cover, zoom, pan, opacity, z            | composite.k (16.16 maps)     | Bevy UI image nodes                          |
      | compositing the overlay                 | composite.k (premultiplied)  | Bevy UI over an un-premultiplied copy        |
      | RGBA to NV12 before the one readback    | nv12buf.k (nv12.k untextured)| none: 8.4 MB of RGBA read back, copied (5 ms) |
      | the proxy sketch of the whole edit      | sketch.k (one dispatch)      | CPU previz                                   |
      | look hypervectors, distances, deltas    | look_grid/proj/ham/pair.k    | CPU (raea-meaning k.rs)                    |
    And on the CPU, on purpose:
      | stage                  | why                                                                                  |
      | the rules (SQL)        | a frame's rows are a few hundred values: 0.2 ms; a dispatch would cost more         |
      | decoding               | VideoToolbox (hardware) hands NV12 over as it is (1,050 fps); openh264 and rav1d are CPU codecs |
      | the Blitz overlay      | a CPU raster, memoised by its rows' digest: 34 draws for 738 frames                  |
      | encoding               | VideoToolbox takes the NV12 readback directly (0.1 ms plane copy)                    |
      | audio                  | the columns mix in 0.3 s for the whole edit                                          |
    And the optional Apple IOSurface zero-copy is built (RAEA_ZEROCOPY=1, direct render, Apple): the decoder hands
      over its IOSurface-backed NV12 buffer untouched, CoreVideo's Metal texture cache wraps its planes as R8 and RG8
      textures inside wgpu, and composite_tex.k (composite.k reading planes as textures, generated by gen.py) composites
      them. Exact: composite_tex.k on the GPU = composite.k on the GPU = its interpreter on every pixel (comptest), and
      the zero-copy render's 738 frames = the engine's, in order and shuffled
    And measured against upload (bench/zerocopy.txt), per frame of the UGC edit:
      | stage                            | copy + upload        | zero-copy             |
      | decode + upload on the CPU       | 2.05-2.44 ms         | 0.65-0.75 ms          |
      | CPU per upload                   | 0.47-0.52 ms         | none (overlays only)  |
      | GPU upload                       | 0.25-0.27 ms         | 0.03 ms (overlays)    |
      | GPU composite                    | 2.00-2.09 ms         | 2.26-2.45 ms          |
      | GPU in all                       | 2.68-2.79 ms         | 2.89-3.13 ms          |
      | render incl. encode              | 191-198 fps          | 190-198 fps           |
    And on the iPhone 13 Pro Max (the orchestrator's runs): frames identical, CPU decode+upload 2.5-2.7 -> 0.62-0.64 ms a
      frame, 173-174 fps vs 169-172 (the encoder's ceiling there too), but about 85 MB more peak whatever the texture cache
      flush or pool size: likely the reader keeping more IOSurface-backed buffers alive
    And so zero-copy frees about 1.5 ms of CPU per frame and costs the GPU about 0.2-0.4 ms (sampling the decoder's
      linear IOSurface planes is slower than the GPU's own buffers); the render stays at VideoToolbox's encoder ceiling
      (about 200 fps), so it's left off by default: it pays where the CPU is the limit (the phone, first-visit scrubs)

  Scenario: Every kernel is exact
    Then GPU = the CPU interpreter of the same K = native Rust, bit for bit, for nv12.k (a real frame through an sRGB
      texture's Unorm view), composite.k (every pixel of a real two-clip frame with an overlay), nv12buf.k, sketch.k's
      parts, and the look kernels on all 738 frames of the take and both b-rolls: grid, projection, all-pairs
      Hamming and a shuffled look_pair (../kgpu/bench/looktest-738.json; the interpreter in 60 s)
    And the K runtime on GPU arrays passes its 38-expression corpus and CPU = GPU on 18 programs x 9 sizes to 1M
    And the dialect gained what the path needed: texture inputs, several outputs, when-blocks (the composite does a
      clip's work only where it covers the pixel: 6.3 -> 3.0 ms), rebinding, integer maths throughout

  Scenario: The new path's frames are exact and close to step 0's
