  Scenario: Portable and bounded
    Then cargo check is green for raea-media (soft, soft+av1, soft+av1+apple) and raea-kgpu on aarch64-apple-darwin,
      aarch64-apple-ios, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu and aarch64-linux-android (bench/g8-crosscheck.txt); the kernels are
      WGSL on wgpu (Metal here, Vulkan and DX12 by construction)
    And the GPU pool holds at most 1 GiB (least recently used out); the render's peak footprint was 1.45 GB
    And later the pool's budget became per use: 32 MiB for a render in frame order (it reuses almost nothing), 1 GiB
      for scrubbing on the Mac, 256 MiB on phones, RAEA_POOL_MB over all: the UGC render to mp4 peaks at 513 MB
      (engine) and 525 MB (direct) at the same speed and with the same 738 frames

    And on the iPhone 13 Pro Max with the final build and no settings (the orchestrator's run): the UGC edit to mp4
      151 fps render, peak 406 MB; its direct render 171 fps incl. encode, peak 540 MB; the 3D hook 161 fps, peak 543 MB
      (the encoder chose real-time for it); the UGC edit's 738 hashes on the phone = the Mac's

  Scenario: Known limits and what's next
    Then a first visit costs a decode and, for a new overlay state, a Blitz raster (30 ms mean); a pre-pass or
      background ingest would make scrubbing warm everywhere
    And edits with 3D content preview through the engine (gpuview's background is the camera's clear colour)
    And bandwidth-bound K verbs on big arrays (+/ over 16M ints) are 2-3x slower on the GPU than on 10 CPU cores here
      (unified memory): the GPU wins on the frame path because the data already lives there
    And the HEVC take still needs its H.264 mezzanine for the portable decoders

  Scenario: The outputs
    Then the edit rendered on the GPU path is examples/out/ugc-g8.mp4 (738 frames, AAC, plays in AVFoundation,
      no ffmpeg decode errors, SSIM 0.980 vs its uncompressed frames)
    And the scrub demo is examples/out/scrub-demo.mp4 (the 150 scrubbed frames, read back from the GPU)
